Some of the finding's examples are wrong. Per my raw-byte decode (confirm with WBT `chorizite-parse-dat-record` on Region 0x13000000 before implementing):
- index 8 is Clear (gets light-snow, -1°C);
- index 9 is Rainy (gets heavy-snow, -5°C);
- index 12 is Cloudy, not Rainy (gets hot-dry, 32°C);
- index 13 is Cloudy (gets cold-clear);
- index 5 is Sunny (gets light-rain);
- index 6 is Sunny (gets thunderstorm, though is_storm is overridden when sky objects are present);
- index 19 is Rainy (gets hail).

The counts Sunny 5 / Cloudy 4 quoted from sky-holtburger-quality-plan.md disagree with my decode (Sunny 6 / Cloudy 3).

Retail 'seasons' are the 12 named months (GameTime seasons, starts 0,30,…,330) and carry no weather or temperature meaning. A month-to-winter/spring/summer/autumn mapping is a non-retail kept-upgrade design choice for the owner, not a parity fix.

Temperature drives frost, cloud base (LCL) and particle gates, so the change is visual.

